## Intuition
- A string of length n has O(n²) substrings.
- The problem asks for the number of distinct substrings, not all substrings.
- Two substrings are identical if and only if their character sequences are identical.


- A Trie (prefix tree) is ideal for:
    - Storing strings
    - Sharing common prefixes
    - Avoiding duplicate storage of identical substrings
## Approach
- Insert all suffixes of the string into a Trie.
- While inserting a suffix, count how many new Trie nodes are created.
- Each newly created node represents a new distinct substring.
- Duplicate substrings reuse existing Trie paths and do not create new nodes.
- Sum all new node creations across suffix insertions.
- Add 1 to include the empty substring.

## Solution
```cpp[]
class TrieNode {
public:
    TrieNode* children[26];

    TrieNode(){
        for(int i=0;i<26;i++){
            children[i] = NULL;
        }
    }
};

class Trie{
    public:
    TrieNode* root;

    Trie(){
        root = new TrieNode();
    }
    int insert(string word, int idx) {
		int cnt = 0;
        TrieNode* curr = root;

        for(int i=idx;i<word.size();i++){
            int idx = word[i] - 'a';

            if(!curr->children[idx]){
				cnt++;
                curr->children[idx] = new TrieNode();
            }
            curr = curr->children[idx];
        }
		return cnt;
    }
};

class Solution{	
	public:
		int countDistinctSubstring(string s){
			Trie* trie = new Trie;

			int cnt = 0;

			string str = s;

			for(int i=0;i<s.size();i++){
				int insertions = trie->insert(str, i);
				cnt += insertions;
			}

			return cnt+1;
		}
};
```

## Complexity
**Time Complexity:** 
O(n^2) - (Inserting n suffixes, each up to length n)

**Space Complexity:**
O(n^2) - worst case: (All substrings are unique)
